1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,160
 Hey everyone, Ryan here and welcome back to our series on prosthodontics.

2
00:00:07,160 --> 00:00:11,600
 Like all my videos, I'm going to be focusing on the highest deal things you need to know

3
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:13,640
 for the board exam.

4
00:00:13,640 --> 00:00:19,440
 So in this video, we're going to talk about jaw relations, occlusion, and how we capture

5
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:26,320
 this information and use it to treatment plan prosthodontic cases.

6
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:31,480
 So first, it's important to talk about how to capture the arrangement of teeth in a

7
00:00:31,480 --> 00:00:32,480
 patient.

8
00:00:32,480 --> 00:00:37,280
 In a future video, I'll talk about all the different impression materials out there,

9
00:00:37,280 --> 00:00:43,720
 but allogenit is certainly the most universally used material, excluding digital impressions,

10
00:00:43,720 --> 00:00:47,280
 and the material of choice for diagnostic casts.

11
00:00:47,280 --> 00:00:50,880
 So we're going to talk about it for just a few minutes here.

12
00:00:50,880 --> 00:00:57,640
 And casts are basically molds of your teeth, and they look a little bit like this.

13
00:00:57,640 --> 00:01:04,880
 So allogenit is made up of several components, sodium or potassium salts of alginic acid,

14
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:11,360
 which react chemically with calcium sulfate to produce insoluble calcium alginit.

15
00:01:11,360 --> 00:01:17,120
 There's this component in the alginit powder called diatomaceous earth, which adds strength

16
00:01:17,120 --> 00:01:24,160
 to the impression material, and tri-sodium phosphate controls the setting reaction.

17
00:01:24,160 --> 00:01:29,160
 That's probably the only compound I would commit to memory for the board exam in case

18
00:01:29,160 --> 00:01:32,040
 that comes up in a question.

19
00:01:32,040 --> 00:01:37,960
 So these three bullet points all have to do with the alginit powder, which you then mix

20
00:01:37,960 --> 00:01:42,840
 with some preset amount of water, depending on the alginit you're using.

21
00:01:42,840 --> 00:01:47,160
 And you want to make sure you have enough bulk of your impression material, because more

22
00:01:47,160 --> 00:01:52,320
 bulk means less susceptibility to unwanted dimensional changes.

23
00:01:52,320 --> 00:01:58,560
 So the alginit should be sufficiently thick in order to capture the anatomy of the patient's

24
00:01:58,560 --> 00:02:00,440
 teeth accurately.

25
00:02:00,440 --> 00:02:05,720
 That's incredibly important for the purposes of taking an impression.

26
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:11,240
 So depending on what impression material you're using, about two to three minutes generally

27
00:02:11,240 --> 00:02:15,520
 is the amount of time that you'll have to keep that impression tray seated in the patient's

28
00:02:15,520 --> 00:02:20,840
 mouth before the impression material sets, and then at which point you can remove that

29
00:02:20,840 --> 00:02:23,960
 tray from the patient's mouth.

30
00:02:23,960 --> 00:02:28,440
 Next you would pour that impression within about 15 minutes.

31
00:02:28,440 --> 00:02:34,680
 By pour the impression, I mean you mix a special powder and water together to make a stone

32
00:02:34,680 --> 00:02:43,240
 paste that we literally pour into this impression, and it hardens to form the moulder cast of

33
00:02:43,240 --> 00:02:46,240
 these teeth that I showed before.

34
00:02:46,240 --> 00:02:52,960
 And then within 30 to 60 minutes, we can actually separate the casts from the impression and

35
00:02:52,960 --> 00:02:59,680
 then throw away the impression, leaving just these casts that we can study and use in some

36
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:02,480
 of the steps that I'll talk about later.

37
00:03:02,480 --> 00:03:07,920
 Alright, so one of the central concepts I'm going to be talking about in this video is

38
00:03:07,920 --> 00:03:13,680
 a concept called Maxillow Mandibular Relations, or MMR for short.

39
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:15,560
 So let's break down this term.

40
00:03:15,560 --> 00:03:22,920
 Maxillow is of course referring to the maxilla, or the upper jaw and its teeth, and mandibular

41
00:03:22,920 --> 00:03:27,160
 referring to the mandible, or the lower jaw and its teeth.

42
00:03:27,160 --> 00:03:34,960
 So MMR is all about the relationship between the upper and lower jaws and their respective

43
00:03:34,960 --> 00:03:36,280
 teeth.

44
00:03:36,280 --> 00:03:41,580
 And there are basically two main relationships in which the maxillow teeth and mandibular

45
00:03:41,580 --> 00:03:44,640
 teeth can be related to one another.

46
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:52,120
 And these are CR and MI, and we'll talk about both of these right now.

47
00:03:52,120 --> 00:04:01,020
 So CR is centric relation, that's what it stands for, and the definition of CR is this

48
00:04:01,020 --> 00:04:04,000
 first bullet point here.

49
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:10,520
 So CR is the position in which condyles articulate with the thinnest A-vascular portion of their

50
00:04:10,520 --> 00:04:18,680
 respective discs in the most anterior superior position against the articular eminences.

51
00:04:18,680 --> 00:04:21,760
 So that's a lot going on there, that's a lot of words.

52
00:04:21,760 --> 00:04:27,200
 And if you could commit this to memory eventually, it would be fantastic, because this definition

53
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:32,040
 is just a perfect representation of what CR is.

54
00:04:32,040 --> 00:04:37,040
 And it does appear on the board exam, and knowing all of these things about it is very,

55
00:04:37,040 --> 00:04:38,480
 very important.

56
00:04:38,480 --> 00:04:43,920
 But even better would be to really understand what it all means.

57
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:50,760
 So I underlined and bolded condyles, because that's what's important with the CR relationship.

58
00:04:50,760 --> 00:04:57,800
 So here in this picture, we have, of course, a condyle, and the jaw would come down here

59
00:04:57,800 --> 00:04:59,440
 into the right.

60
00:04:59,440 --> 00:05:05,600
 And up here we have the glenoid fossa, and then the articular eminence coming down this

61
00:05:05,600 --> 00:05:07,280
 way.

62
00:05:07,280 --> 00:05:14,520
 So this is the temporal mandibular joint complex, and all of the things that it contains.

63
00:05:14,520 --> 00:05:22,080
 So going back to the definition here, I'm going to get my pen ready.

64
00:05:22,080 --> 00:05:28,160
 It's talking about the condyles articulating with the thinnest A-vascular portion of their

65
00:05:28,160 --> 00:05:29,400
 respective discs.

66
00:05:29,400 --> 00:05:33,600
 So of course there are two condyles, one on the right and one on the left side of our

67
00:05:33,600 --> 00:05:42,200
 mandible, and they each are articulating with this disc here.

68
00:05:42,200 --> 00:05:47,440
 And so I will outline the disc with this red pen here.

69
00:05:47,440 --> 00:05:57,160
 So this beige-colored structure is the articular disc that the condyle is constantly in contact

70
00:05:57,160 --> 00:05:59,600
 with.

71
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:05,440
 And the definition is referring to the fact that centric relation is when the condyles

72
00:06:05,440 --> 00:06:13,040
 are in the most anterior and superior position.

73
00:06:13,040 --> 00:06:17,560
 And that's what those arrows are referring to here.

74
00:06:17,560 --> 00:06:22,880
 And you can tell that this portion of the disc, in contrast to this portion and this

75
00:06:22,880 --> 00:06:27,320
 portion, is the thinnest portion of the articular disc.

76
00:06:27,320 --> 00:06:33,280
 So this is a very good representation of what centric relation looks like where the

77
00:06:33,280 --> 00:06:42,240
 condyle is in some ways at home or at rest, and it's in the most anterior and superior

78
00:06:42,240 --> 00:06:47,360
 position articulating with the thinnest portion of the disc.

79
00:06:47,360 --> 00:06:52,800
 Now notice I didn't mention anything about the teeth in this scenario.

80
00:06:52,800 --> 00:06:57,800
 And this image in fact doesn't have any teeth depicted on it.

81
00:06:57,800 --> 00:07:02,720
 And that's because the teeth really aren't important in centric relation.

82
00:07:02,720 --> 00:07:05,160
 It's all about the condyles.

83
00:07:05,160 --> 00:07:10,400
 So centric relation is completely independent of teeth.

84
00:07:10,400 --> 00:07:16,400
 And I think this is a very helpful way to think about centric relation, especially when

85
00:07:16,400 --> 00:07:22,840
 we contrast it to maximum intercussation, which is our MI.

86
00:07:22,840 --> 00:07:30,560
 That's our other big, big position in terms of maximum and abular relations.

87
00:07:30,560 --> 00:07:35,280
 So maximum intercussation, also sometimes referred to as centric occlusion, I want to

88
00:07:35,280 --> 00:07:42,240
 include that as well, has to do with the complete interdigitation of the teeth.

89
00:07:42,240 --> 00:07:50,560
 So this is in complete contrast to centric relation, even though they might look similar

90
00:07:50,560 --> 00:07:54,440
 clinically if you were just to look at their smile, and we'll talk about how to differentiate

91
00:07:54,440 --> 00:07:55,920
 that in a second.

92
00:07:55,920 --> 00:07:59,280
 But maximum intercussation is all about the teeth.

93
00:07:59,280 --> 00:08:02,520
 We want the teeth to be completely locked together.

94
00:08:02,520 --> 00:08:07,880
 So the teeth are at home, and the condyle, we don't really care about that anymore.

95
00:08:07,880 --> 00:08:13,240
 So whereas in centric relation, it was all about the condyle, and we were not really

96
00:08:13,240 --> 00:08:18,000
 concerned about where the teeth were, maximum intercussation is the opposite.

97
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:21,080
 This is where the teeth are locked together, and we don't really care about where the

98
00:08:21,080 --> 00:08:23,720
 condyles are.

99
00:08:23,720 --> 00:08:28,080
 That might be a little bit of an oversimplification, but I think it really, really helps to put

100
00:08:28,080 --> 00:08:33,320
 this all together, big picture thinking, especially for the board's exam.

101
00:08:33,320 --> 00:08:39,120
 All right, so let's talk a little bit more about the distinctions between these two positions.

102
00:08:39,120 --> 00:08:43,720
 So maximum intercussation and centric relation, which I'll just refer to you from now on as

103
00:08:43,720 --> 00:08:53,320
 MI and CR, for both of our sakes, so MI and CR coincide in only 10% of the population.

104
00:08:53,320 --> 00:09:01,000
 That means 90% of patients have a different CR where the condyles are at rest and MI position

105
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:09,160
 where the teeth are at rest, and in these 90% of people, there's actually a slide in centric.

106
00:09:09,160 --> 00:09:14,560
 That means where the teeth slide together between these two positions.

107
00:09:14,560 --> 00:09:20,120
 Now some of my earliest videos on this channel that I made several years ago cover this topic

108
00:09:20,120 --> 00:09:24,040
 of occlusion and Poisson's diagram fairly comprehensively.

109
00:09:24,040 --> 00:09:30,000
 So I'll leave links in the description for both of those occlusion videos.

110
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:35,080
 Now casts of the teeth can be mounted on an articulator, which we will cover later in

111
00:09:35,080 --> 00:09:40,720
 this video, in either of these two positions, CR or MI.

112
00:09:40,720 --> 00:09:48,520
 We use MI particularly or generally when a single crown or bridge is done, and the interlocking

113
00:09:48,520 --> 00:09:54,240
 of teeth can be maintained before and after the procedure.

114
00:09:54,240 --> 00:10:01,120
 So if the patient's teeth really lock together well, and you can reliably do what's called

115
00:10:01,120 --> 00:10:07,280
 hand articulation, where you put the two casts together just by hand and you can kind of

116
00:10:07,280 --> 00:10:12,960
 feel the teeth locked together and be pretty stable in a certain position, that's when

117
00:10:12,960 --> 00:10:19,760
 MI could be a very good way to mount the casts, again which we'll talk about in just a second.

118
00:10:19,760 --> 00:10:29,040
 Now CR is used for mounting casts in nearly every other situation, and CR is so important

119
00:10:29,040 --> 00:10:34,760
 because what if you have a patient without any teeth, which we would call fully edentulous,

120
00:10:34,760 --> 00:10:42,080
 or what if there's a patient that has lots of posterior teeth missing, or with lots of

121
00:10:42,080 --> 00:10:48,040
 malalignment or class 3 bite issues.

122
00:10:48,040 --> 00:10:54,300
 CR is better to use and more reliable in these situations, and particularly thinking back

123
00:10:54,300 --> 00:10:59,480
 to the patient without any teeth, well they don't have an MI position because they have

124
00:10:59,480 --> 00:11:04,240
 no teeth, but they still have condyles, and that's what's important.

125
00:11:04,240 --> 00:11:11,040
 So for those patients where MI is impossible to maintain, like in complete dentures, or

126
00:11:11,040 --> 00:11:16,480
 we're doing a full mouth reconstruction where every tooth is going to become crown and bridge

127
00:11:16,480 --> 00:11:23,520
 work, CR is better to use and more reliable than MI because their teeth may not lock together

128
00:11:23,520 --> 00:11:29,560
 in a particular repeatable position, or maybe they have no teeth at all.

129
00:11:29,560 --> 00:11:38,400
 And that's just it, CR is the most reliable and reproducible jaw position of the mouth,

130
00:11:38,400 --> 00:11:43,960
 and that's super super important to know for all of prosthodontics, particularly for this

131
00:11:43,960 --> 00:11:47,600
 section of the board exam.

132
00:11:47,600 --> 00:11:53,800
 So what we've been dancing around so far is the concept of occlusal harmony, and I love

133
00:11:53,800 --> 00:12:00,080
 this analogy so much, and that's the joints, muscles, and teeth must function in harmony

134
00:12:00,080 --> 00:12:03,000
 much like a door in its frame.

135
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:08,040
 So you can think of this analogy extending to each component of the mouth and the temporal

136
00:12:08,040 --> 00:12:10,360
 mandibular joint complex.

137
00:12:10,360 --> 00:12:14,680
 So the hinges of the door would be the TMJ.

138
00:12:14,680 --> 00:12:20,440
 The teeth are like the door and its frame, the frame being the stationary component which

139
00:12:20,440 --> 00:12:26,080
 is the maxilla, and the door being the movable component which is the mandible.

140
00:12:26,080 --> 00:12:32,400
 And the muscles of this complex are like the hands that you and I are using to open and

141
00:12:32,400 --> 00:12:34,680
 close the door.

142
00:12:34,680 --> 00:12:39,600
 So sometimes the door has a corner that's a little too long and the door doesn't close

143
00:12:39,600 --> 00:12:45,840
 all the way or the frame is bent just slightly, one of the hinges is off-kilter, or maybe

144
00:12:45,840 --> 00:12:49,960
 we slam the door too hard and just broke the thing.

145
00:12:49,960 --> 00:12:55,120
 And I just love this analogy because you can use all of those little ideas and concepts

146
00:12:55,120 --> 00:13:03,000
 to think about what goes wrong in a particular component of the joint muscles and teeth.

147
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:10,880
 So we want everything to work together in occlusal harmony.

148
00:13:10,880 --> 00:13:16,000
 So one of the questions you may have is well how do you get a patient to the CR position?

149
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:21,320
 I understand how to get them to MI position where their teeth lock together but the CR

150
00:13:21,320 --> 00:13:24,640
 part is a little bit harder to understand.

151
00:13:24,640 --> 00:13:27,080
 And this is where we're going to talk about it.

152
00:13:27,080 --> 00:13:35,320
 So if you just tell the patient to bite together that's probably going to be their MI position.

153
00:13:35,320 --> 00:13:39,960
 Or maybe they have several different bites that are just not repeatable and they bite

154
00:13:39,960 --> 00:13:43,760
 differently every time you ask them to bite together.

155
00:13:43,760 --> 00:13:50,520
 So the CR position may not be that patients at home position particularly if their MI

156
00:13:50,520 --> 00:13:57,160
 and CR positions don't coincide, remember that's 90% of patients.

157
00:13:57,160 --> 00:14:04,120
 So one of the most accurate methods to obtain an accurate CR inter occlusal record is with

158
00:14:04,120 --> 00:14:07,240
 bimanual manipulation.

159
00:14:07,240 --> 00:14:14,720
 And the inter occlusal record portion I'm talking about is made with either wax or polyvinyl

160
00:14:14,720 --> 00:14:21,760
 saloxane which is also known as PBS and it's a registration or physical capturing of the

161
00:14:21,760 --> 00:14:27,320
 relationship between the upper and lower teeth made with an elastomeric material like

162
00:14:27,320 --> 00:14:30,280
 PBS for instance.

163
00:14:30,280 --> 00:14:35,280
 And it looks something like this, the patient would essentially bite together into it, this

164
00:14:35,280 --> 00:14:41,240
 material would set and later would allow you to mount your diagnostic casts together and

165
00:14:41,240 --> 00:14:45,580
 ideally that same relationship.

166
00:14:45,580 --> 00:14:49,920
 So with the patient lying back like in this picture support the posterior mandible with

167
00:14:49,920 --> 00:14:53,680
 fingers and the chin with the thumbs.

168
00:14:53,680 --> 00:14:59,200
 And your idea with bimanual manipulation is to deprogram the jaw.

169
00:14:59,200 --> 00:15:05,000
 What this means is basically have the patient relax their jaw as much as possible so you

170
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:12,080
 can manipulate it in your hands and you want to gently direct their jaw so the condyles

171
00:15:12,080 --> 00:15:19,800
 are in the most superior and anterior position because that's what centric relation was all

172
00:15:19,800 --> 00:15:21,040
 about.

173
00:15:21,040 --> 00:15:27,880
 And then once you can access that centric relation position you can swing the mandible until you

174
00:15:27,880 --> 00:15:31,560
 get one tooth contact.

175
00:15:31,560 --> 00:15:37,520
 So that's what I'm talking about and I say identify the first CR tooth contact and repeat

176
00:15:37,520 --> 00:15:42,120
 until you identify a consistent first tooth contact.

177
00:15:42,120 --> 00:15:49,000
 Because since 90% of patients don't have the same MI and CR, CR is likely not going to be

178
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:52,900
 where all the teeth just lock together instantaneously.

179
00:15:52,900 --> 00:15:58,280
 You'll have the condyles in their centric relation position and then as you close the

180
00:15:58,280 --> 00:16:04,880
 mandible there might be one or two or maybe three teeth that contact first with an opposing

181
00:16:04,880 --> 00:16:10,640
 tooth and if you can consistently reproduce those first tooth contacts you know you're

182
00:16:10,640 --> 00:16:12,600
 in CR.

183
00:16:12,600 --> 00:16:18,680
 So that you can do a couple times and then what you do at that point the ideal CR record

184
00:16:18,680 --> 00:16:23,920
 would involve keeping the teeth slightly apart in the CR position and then finally taking

185
00:16:23,920 --> 00:16:29,480
 a record of the back teeth with that previous material so you might only capture the posterior

186
00:16:29,480 --> 00:16:34,840
 teeth say premolars and molars for an ideal CR because you don't want the anterior teeth

187
00:16:34,840 --> 00:16:38,880
 to get in the way.

188
00:16:38,880 --> 00:16:43,440
 So we've talked about capturing the teeth of the patient with the alginate and capturing

189
00:16:43,440 --> 00:16:50,280
 how they bite together with the CR/MI record but we're missing one more piece of information

190
00:16:50,280 --> 00:16:57,240
 before we can mount this case on an articulator and that is the face bow record.

191
00:16:57,240 --> 00:17:02,600
 So the objective with the face bow record is to duplicate on the articulator the relationship

192
00:17:02,600 --> 00:17:09,120
 of the maxillary arch to the skull and indirectly the mandible to the rotational center of the

193
00:17:09,120 --> 00:17:13,440
 TMJs that exists for that particular patient.

194
00:17:13,440 --> 00:17:22,320
 Okay so another bunch of lots of words here so let's define and simplify this.

195
00:17:22,320 --> 00:17:29,440
 So this is a face bow it is basically a conglomeration of a bunch of mechanical components that

196
00:17:29,440 --> 00:17:37,040
 make various measurements and that face bow would hook up onto an articulator here and

197
00:17:37,040 --> 00:17:45,840
 tell you where that maxillary cast should go relative to the hinge axis of the articulator

198
00:17:45,840 --> 00:17:49,240
 and there are two different kinds of face bows.

199
00:17:49,240 --> 00:17:55,480
 We have the arbitrary face bow which orients the maxillary cast to the skull via the external

200
00:17:55,480 --> 00:18:02,880
 auditory meat edus to stabilize the bow essentially the face bow is fit into the patient's ears

201
00:18:02,880 --> 00:18:08,360
 like earbuds whereas the kinematic face bow is a little bit more complex and it's placed

202
00:18:08,360 --> 00:18:13,040
 directly on the hinge axis of the mandible.

203
00:18:13,040 --> 00:18:16,840
 So you can think of the arbitrary face bow being a little bit less precise but more

204
00:18:16,840 --> 00:18:18,720
 easy to use.

205
00:18:18,720 --> 00:18:24,720
 So basically how I think of it is the face bow record transfers the relationship between

206
00:18:24,720 --> 00:18:30,840
 the maxilla and the rest of the skull from the patient to the articulator.

207
00:18:30,840 --> 00:18:35,880
 So we know where the maxillary cast or the upper cast is going to be positioned where

208
00:18:35,880 --> 00:18:38,280
 we mount it onto the articulator.

209
00:18:38,280 --> 00:18:43,440
 So next let's talk about articulators in the next slide.

210
00:18:43,440 --> 00:18:48,800
 So I love this, well I love both of these pictures actually because this one in particular

211
00:18:48,800 --> 00:18:54,680
 shows how the articulator functions as an analog to the human skull with the maxilla

212
00:18:54,680 --> 00:18:56,600
 and mandible and all of that.

213
00:18:56,600 --> 00:19:02,960
 So the upper member of the articulator that's this component here and this component in

214
00:19:02,960 --> 00:19:08,720
 blue on this image is an analog to the patient's maxilla.

215
00:19:08,720 --> 00:19:15,000
 The lower member which is this part here and this part in yellow is an analog to the patient's

216
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:22,360
 mandible and then you have the hinge axis of the articulator which is an analog to the

217
00:19:22,360 --> 00:19:24,760
 patient's TMJ.

218
00:19:24,760 --> 00:19:28,680
 So it's really cool how this all works together.

219
00:19:28,680 --> 00:19:33,480
 Again the face bow record transfers the relationship between maxilla and the rest of the skull

220
00:19:33,480 --> 00:19:36,440
 from the patient to the articulator.

221
00:19:36,440 --> 00:19:41,340
 So you use the face bow to tell you where this maxillary cast is going to go.

222
00:19:41,340 --> 00:19:44,680
 Is it going to go a little bit forward, a little bit more back, a little up, a little

223
00:19:44,680 --> 00:19:47,320
 tilted or canted one way or the other?

224
00:19:47,320 --> 00:19:50,800
 That's what the face bow is for.

225
00:19:50,800 --> 00:19:56,600
 The inter occlusal record or the byte registration that we talked about previously transfers

226
00:19:56,600 --> 00:20:03,320
 the relationship between maxilla and mandible from the patient to the articulator.

227
00:20:03,320 --> 00:20:09,040
 So you use the record to mount the mandibular cast to the maxillary cast after you use the

228
00:20:09,040 --> 00:20:13,320
 face bow and this would be like a wax byte registration.

229
00:20:13,320 --> 00:20:19,400
 So you could use this once you have the maxillary cast in place from the face bow to then mount

230
00:20:19,400 --> 00:20:24,280
 the mandibular cast to the maxillary cast.

231
00:20:24,280 --> 00:20:26,140
 And then you have your mounting.

232
00:20:26,140 --> 00:20:31,600
 It's a physical representation of your patient's byte outside the mouth which is incredibly

233
00:20:31,600 --> 00:20:38,240
 useful to us as dentists and prosthodontists doing prosthetic work, doing operative procedures.

234
00:20:38,240 --> 00:20:43,120
 This is just something that really can't be beat and articulators and mounted cases are

235
00:20:43,120 --> 00:20:46,800
 so incredibly useful.

236
00:20:46,800 --> 00:20:51,400
 So let's talk a little bit about the different types of articulators.

237
00:20:51,400 --> 00:20:56,360
 So we have the non-adjustable articulator first and we're basically going to go from

238
00:20:56,360 --> 00:21:00,600
 the most simple form of articulator to the most complex.

239
00:21:00,600 --> 00:21:07,920
 So non-adjustable articulator does not reproduce the full range of mandibular movement.

240
00:21:07,920 --> 00:21:11,560
 Basically it opens up and down much like a door hinge.

241
00:21:11,560 --> 00:21:19,480
 Or as we know that the patient and their mouth functions much more complex than that.

242
00:21:19,480 --> 00:21:22,640
 There's a lot more dynamic movement that goes on.

243
00:21:22,640 --> 00:21:29,040
 And again this is sort of demonstrated in Pissalt's diagram which I talked about in my

244
00:21:29,040 --> 00:21:31,080
 first occlusion videos.

245
00:21:31,080 --> 00:21:35,960
 We know the mandible is a lot more dynamic than just opening and closing.

246
00:21:35,960 --> 00:21:41,520
 There's a rotation phase followed by a sliding phase and this articulator just simply can

247
00:21:41,520 --> 00:21:46,720
 not replicate the complications of mandibular movement.

248
00:21:46,720 --> 00:21:50,880
 So it falls short in that regard just a little bit.

249
00:21:50,880 --> 00:21:56,920
 Also the distance between the hinge and the teeth is significantly shorter in the patient.

250
00:21:56,920 --> 00:22:02,280
 So you can see here that the distance between you know where the teeth would go somewhere

251
00:22:02,280 --> 00:22:13,400
 here let's say and the hinge axis over here is much shorter than what would be in the patient.

252
00:22:13,400 --> 00:22:20,360
 And so that's just another shortcoming of the non-adjustable articulator.

253
00:22:20,360 --> 00:22:26,440
 And as a result this may result in premature contacts and incorrect ridge and groove direction

254
00:22:26,440 --> 00:22:28,480
 of the restorations.

255
00:22:28,480 --> 00:22:33,280
 So unless you're doing a very very simple case everything is mounted in MI, don't have

256
00:22:33,280 --> 00:22:39,600
 many concerns about premature contacts, occlusion concerns, maybe you can get away with a non-adjustable

257
00:22:39,600 --> 00:22:41,520
 articulator.

258
00:22:41,520 --> 00:22:47,080
 If you can't then much preferred would be the semi-adjustable articulator which is a notch

259
00:22:47,080 --> 00:22:49,680
 above that one.

260
00:22:49,680 --> 00:22:53,920
 So this one adds a bit more customization let's say.

261
00:22:53,920 --> 00:23:00,760
 It allows you to set the Bennet angle and the horizontal condler inclination or HCI.

262
00:23:00,760 --> 00:23:06,520
 The classic numbers to remember for the board exam are 15 degrees for Bennet angle, 30 degrees

263
00:23:06,520 --> 00:23:09,240
 for HCI.

264
00:23:09,240 --> 00:23:15,520
 So I again cover these concepts in my occlusion videos but just as a refresher the horizontal

265
00:23:15,520 --> 00:23:21,760
 condler inclination is basically the vertical angle that condiles move as the patient's

266
00:23:21,760 --> 00:23:28,240
 jaws function, the condiles slide down the articular eminences and Bennet angle is essentially

267
00:23:28,240 --> 00:23:34,960
 the horizontal angle as the condiles move laterally in function.

268
00:23:34,960 --> 00:23:42,040
 The Archon articulator and non-archon articulator are two distinct categories that we start

269
00:23:42,040 --> 00:23:47,120
 running into when we talk about semi-adjustable articulators.

270
00:23:47,120 --> 00:23:55,120
 So the Archon articulator is where the condiles are part of the lower member and the fossa

271
00:23:55,120 --> 00:23:57,520
 are part of the upper member.

272
00:23:57,520 --> 00:24:03,760
 So when I'm talking about with the condiles in this image here we can see this little

273
00:24:03,760 --> 00:24:10,680
 gold ball here is sort of the analog to the patient's condile if you will and that's

274
00:24:10,680 --> 00:24:15,280
 directly attached to this lower member which we saw as part of the mandible.

275
00:24:15,280 --> 00:24:22,360
 So that's pretty anatomically accurate where the condiles part of the mandibular bone and

276
00:24:22,360 --> 00:24:25,120
 then the fossa is where that golden ball sits.

277
00:24:25,120 --> 00:24:33,000
 If I can erase that writing there you can see how this silver portion with the gold slot

278
00:24:33,000 --> 00:24:39,360
 here would be the analog to the patient's Glenoid fossa and that's part of the upper

279
00:24:39,360 --> 00:24:47,740
 member so this is a loyal and accurate anatomical analog to the patient.

280
00:24:47,740 --> 00:24:50,480
 So that's why we call it Archon.

281
00:24:50,480 --> 00:24:58,800
 The non-archon example is the opposite where it's not anatomically correct and in fact

282
00:24:58,800 --> 00:25:02,440
 the upper and lower members are rigidly attached.

283
00:25:02,440 --> 00:25:08,240
 So those are two terms I would be familiar with and this one again is an example of the

284
00:25:08,240 --> 00:25:16,520
 Archon semi-adjustable articulator and all those the HCI you can change here and the

285
00:25:16,520 --> 00:25:22,240
 Benet angle you can change here so there's a lot more customization than with the non-adjustable

286
00:25:22,240 --> 00:25:27,680
 articulator where it's just a simple open and close hinge.

287
00:25:27,680 --> 00:25:34,920
 And then we have the most complex articulator, the fully adjustable articulator and this

288
00:25:34,920 --> 00:25:42,280
 one has even more things that you can adjust lots of things you can mess with back here

289
00:25:42,280 --> 00:25:46,600
 and this goes a little bit beyond the scope of this video I don't think you really need

290
00:25:46,600 --> 00:25:50,200
 to know how this works or anything like that.

291
00:25:50,200 --> 00:25:55,560
 There's this very complex tracing process involving a pantograph which is used to follow

292
00:25:55,560 --> 00:26:01,200
 the patient's exact border movements and again I don't think you would need to know this

293
00:26:01,200 --> 00:26:07,560
 for the board exam but just for completeness sake this is the most complex articulator out

294
00:26:07,560 --> 00:26:09,840
 of the three we talked about.

295
00:26:09,840 --> 00:26:16,120
 Alright so one thing I did want to mention particularly for the board exam is that casts

296
00:26:16,120 --> 00:26:23,440
 poured from Alginet are more accurately mounted with wax records and casts poured from elastomeric

297
00:26:23,440 --> 00:26:28,840
 materials like the PBS that purple material we talked about and it comes in many different

298
00:26:28,840 --> 00:26:36,280
 colors are more accurately mounted with elastomeric materials or zinc oxide eugenol paste.

299
00:26:36,280 --> 00:26:40,900
 So this is something that one of those kind of random dental material facts that I would

300
00:26:40,900 --> 00:26:46,600
 commit to memory for the board exam but the nice thing here is that if you pour the casts

301
00:26:46,600 --> 00:26:54,240
 from an elastomeric impression it's best to mount them with the same kind of material.

302
00:26:54,240 --> 00:27:00,840
 Alright so if occlusion is all about teeth functioning together, disclusion is where

303
00:27:00,840 --> 00:27:06,800
 the teeth are separating from one another in function and that's to in order to protect

304
00:27:06,800 --> 00:27:14,240
 the teeth from wearing and receiving too much occlusal force and potential trauma.

305
00:27:14,240 --> 00:27:18,560
 So let's talk a little bit about disclusion because this is really important or talking

306
00:27:18,560 --> 00:27:26,320
 about making prosthetic teeth and using our mounted cases in order to determine how to

307
00:27:26,320 --> 00:27:31,880
 build up those prosthetic teeth.

308
00:27:31,880 --> 00:27:37,480
 So again this slide is all about disclusion when the patient is protruding the lower jaw

309
00:27:37,480 --> 00:27:40,440
 forward as shown in this image here.

310
00:27:40,440 --> 00:27:47,280
 So these two concepts have to do with the patient protruding the jaw straight forward.

311
00:27:47,280 --> 00:27:55,880
 So we have one posterior determinant of occlusion and one anterior determinant of occlusion.

312
00:27:55,880 --> 00:28:01,600
 And these two concepts are extremely important for the board exam.

313
00:28:01,600 --> 00:28:07,960
 So condler guidance has to do with the condiles which are of course in the posterior portion

314
00:28:07,960 --> 00:28:09,200
 of the mouth.

315
00:28:09,200 --> 00:28:13,280
 So it is the posterior determinant of occlusion.

316
00:28:13,280 --> 00:28:19,720
 And this has to do with the slope of the articular eminence and it's represented by that horizontal

317
00:28:19,720 --> 00:28:25,400
 condler inclination that we just briefly mentioned before on the articulator.

318
00:28:25,400 --> 00:28:31,320
 So let me get my pen out here again and highlight for you.

319
00:28:31,320 --> 00:28:37,840
 This is the slope of the articular eminence that when the condyle is or when the jaw is

320
00:28:37,840 --> 00:28:45,280
 being protruded forward the condyle is being forced to slide down this articular eminence.

321
00:28:45,280 --> 00:28:48,800
 So depending on the patient they may have a different slope.

322
00:28:48,800 --> 00:28:53,880
 Some might be more shallow and some might be steeper.

323
00:28:53,880 --> 00:28:59,240
 And in a future video I'll talk specifically about all of these determinants of occlusion

324
00:28:59,240 --> 00:29:03,280
 because there's definitely a way that we can simplify it to remember just what you need

325
00:29:03,280 --> 00:29:06,240
 to know for the board exam and it's really really helpful.

326
00:29:06,240 --> 00:29:08,640
 So I'll definitely talk more about that then.

327
00:29:08,640 --> 00:29:14,400
 But there are certain things you have to remember if the articular eminence is shallower or steeper

328
00:29:14,400 --> 00:29:19,320
 and how that would impact the teeth that you could be restoring.

329
00:29:19,320 --> 00:29:25,640
 So anyway, you can think of it like this is sort of limiting the movement of the jaw

330
00:29:25,640 --> 00:29:31,480
 in a certain way because the condiles have really nowhere else to go but to be guided

331
00:29:31,480 --> 00:29:33,520
 by the articular eminence.

332
00:29:33,520 --> 00:29:37,680
 So that's a determinant of where the teeth are going to be touching and where they're

333
00:29:37,680 --> 00:29:42,680
 not going to be touching because the condiles really have nowhere else to go but use the

334
00:29:42,680 --> 00:29:46,560
 articular eminence as a guide rail.

335
00:29:46,560 --> 00:29:52,080
 Now we also have incisal guidance which since the incisors are in the anterior portion of

336
00:29:52,080 --> 00:29:55,920
 the mouth that's the anterior determinant of occlusion.

337
00:29:55,920 --> 00:30:02,240
 And this is where the incisal edges of lower incisors are being again forced to move a

338
00:30:02,240 --> 00:30:06,700
 certain way against the lingual slopes of the upper incisors.

339
00:30:06,700 --> 00:30:17,000
 So I'll quickly grab a new color and excuse my drawing here but I will draw a cross section

340
00:30:17,000 --> 00:30:19,080
 of an anterior tooth.

341
00:30:19,080 --> 00:30:26,440
 This is like an incisor with the singulum here and we'll say a lower incisor here with

342
00:30:26,440 --> 00:30:28,840
 a smaller singulum there.

343
00:30:28,840 --> 00:30:34,880
 So these are our two teeth upper and lower and as you bite together and say this lower

344
00:30:34,880 --> 00:30:47,120
 incisors contacting somewhere on that singulum, let us get a green pen and the patients in

345
00:30:47,120 --> 00:30:57,680
 say MI for instance their teeth are completely interdigitated and this lower incisor needs

346
00:30:57,680 --> 00:31:01,360
 to slide forward as the jaw is moving forward.

347
00:31:01,360 --> 00:31:07,400
 Well if the point of that incisor is here it really has nowhere else to go but to be guided

348
00:31:07,400 --> 00:31:11,720
 by this tooth and the lingual surface of that incisor.

349
00:31:11,720 --> 00:31:19,280
 It can't just break right through that incisor so you can see how this is kind of an interesting

350
00:31:19,280 --> 00:31:27,120
 situation where we have a slope from this tooth and a slope from this articular eminence.

351
00:31:27,120 --> 00:31:32,980
 They can be in harmony sometimes not and so these two determinants of occlusion again

352
00:31:32,980 --> 00:31:38,880
 determine where the jaw will move at what angle the jaw will protrude forward and down

353
00:31:38,880 --> 00:31:42,800
 as the mandible is moved forward.

354
00:31:42,800 --> 00:31:48,800
 And if we again go back to the amylog how all of this is related back to the articulator

355
00:31:48,800 --> 00:31:53,760
 we have this thing called a pin and a guide table on the articulator that we can use to

356
00:31:53,760 --> 00:31:56,240
 represent incisal guidance.

357
00:31:56,240 --> 00:32:03,080
 And I'll just mention that briefly in a following slide.

358
00:32:03,080 --> 00:32:10,320
 All right and just to finish up our conversation on disclusion we also have a discluding that's

359
00:32:10,320 --> 00:32:14,000
 occurring when the patient is moving from side to side.

360
00:32:14,000 --> 00:32:18,560
 So this slide was all about moving the jaw forward.

361
00:32:18,560 --> 00:32:24,600
 This slide is all about moving or this concept of canine guidance is all about moving the

362
00:32:24,600 --> 00:32:26,940
 jaw from side to side.

363
00:32:26,940 --> 00:32:33,760
 So when in lateral movements all posterior teeth are ideally immediately excluded as

364
00:32:33,760 --> 00:32:39,720
 contact occurs solely between the upper and lower canine on the working side.

365
00:32:39,720 --> 00:32:44,120
 So this is an ideal situation that doesn't always happen in every patient's for sure

366
00:32:44,120 --> 00:32:48,920
 but the ideal scenario is that when the patient is sliding one way or the other either right

367
00:32:48,920 --> 00:32:56,320
 or left the canines are contacting together and discluding are separating all these posterior

368
00:32:56,320 --> 00:32:59,360
 teeth so they don't have to feel this force.

369
00:32:59,360 --> 00:33:04,640
 The canines are very well equipped they have the longest roots of all the teeth and they're

370
00:33:04,640 --> 00:33:09,240
 built in such a way to be able to handle that stress.

371
00:33:09,240 --> 00:33:15,960
 So that is the concept behind canine guidance and of course the working side is the side

372
00:33:15,960 --> 00:33:23,160
 that the mandible is moving towards that's either right or left anterior guidance is sometimes

373
00:33:23,160 --> 00:33:28,360
 used synonymously with canine guidance but there's an important distinction to be had

374
00:33:28,360 --> 00:33:33,920
 here particularly when you're reading these terms on the board exam I want to make clear

375
00:33:33,920 --> 00:33:43,760
 that anterior guidance refers to both incisal guidance that was with the lower incisors gliding

376
00:33:43,760 --> 00:33:51,320
 against the lingual surfaces of the upper incisors that's in a protrusive movement and canine

377
00:33:51,320 --> 00:33:58,040
 guidance which is this lateral exclusion where the canines are only contacting discluding

378
00:33:58,040 --> 00:34:00,360
 the posterior teeth.

379
00:34:00,360 --> 00:34:06,280
 So anterior guidance because the canines and incisors together make up the anterior teeth

380
00:34:06,280 --> 00:34:10,840
 is all the guidance that occurs between those two units.

381
00:34:10,840 --> 00:34:15,160
 So I think that's a super helpful distinction there.

382
00:34:15,160 --> 00:34:22,240
 So as a summary during protrusive the incisal and condler guidance anterior and posterior

383
00:34:22,240 --> 00:34:27,600
 determinants of occlusion together provide clearance for all the posterior teeth so they're

384
00:34:27,600 --> 00:34:32,120
 separated as the jaw is moving forward.

385
00:34:32,120 --> 00:34:37,440
 During lateral canines on the working side and the condyle on the balancing side which

386
00:34:37,440 --> 00:34:43,280
 we didn't talk about before provide clearance for posterior teeth on the balancing side

387
00:34:43,280 --> 00:34:46,640
 that's the side that the mandibles moving away from.

388
00:34:46,640 --> 00:34:53,520
 So this is such a cool concept because as we'll talk about it's really this concept

389
00:34:53,520 --> 00:34:58,760
 of mutual protection where the front teeth protect the back teeth and the back teeth

390
00:34:58,760 --> 00:35:00,240
 to protect the front teeth.

391
00:35:00,240 --> 00:35:06,600
 It's just such a cool mechanism of occlusion and disclusion that is designed so carefully

392
00:35:06,600 --> 00:35:11,240
 with how the teeth work together.

393
00:35:11,240 --> 00:35:15,840
 So I mentioned briefly this idea of a guide table.

394
00:35:15,840 --> 00:35:21,000
 This is where this is the guide table of the articulator we were looking at before the

395
00:35:21,000 --> 00:35:28,480
 semi-adjustable articulator and this is a mechanical one and this is a custom one.

396
00:35:28,480 --> 00:35:32,920
 And I'll just talk about the difference between those really quickly.

397
00:35:32,920 --> 00:35:37,520
 The posterior guidance must be preserved when restorative procedures change the surfaces

398
00:35:37,520 --> 00:35:39,800
 of any guiding teeth.

399
00:35:39,800 --> 00:35:45,840
 So if you're doing full anterior crowns well that's going to affect the guiding teeth so

400
00:35:45,840 --> 00:35:50,840
 we want to make sure that our anterior determinant of occlusion stays consistent.

401
00:35:50,840 --> 00:35:56,160
 In other words if we're changing the canines we're replacing them with crowns we want to

402
00:35:56,160 --> 00:36:02,400
 make sure those surfaces are true to the surfaces that were there before at some point so we

403
00:36:02,400 --> 00:36:07,860
 can maintain canine guidance if that's what the patient had originally or if that patient

404
00:36:07,860 --> 00:36:12,480
 had a certain slope on the lingual surfaces of the anterior teeth and that was helping

405
00:36:12,480 --> 00:36:16,800
 to disclude the posterior teeth well we want to maintain that too if we're replacing those

406
00:36:16,800 --> 00:36:22,000
 with crowns and this guide table is going to help us with that.

407
00:36:22,000 --> 00:36:27,320
 So the mechanical incisal guide table gives insufficient information to reproduce the

408
00:36:27,320 --> 00:36:32,780
 exact lingual contours of the maxillary anterior teeth because the lingual contours of those

409
00:36:32,780 --> 00:36:38,480
 teeth are curved they're not straight across so the mechanical guide table can only do

410
00:36:38,480 --> 00:36:44,880
 straight lines whereas this one can be curved and more accurately represent the patient's

411
00:36:44,880 --> 00:36:47,360
 natural anatomy.

412
00:36:47,360 --> 00:36:51,800
 And the custom incisal guide table is made out of acrylic resin and is able to provide

413
00:36:51,800 --> 00:36:57,360
 this information because whereas this one we can't customize this one we can customize

414
00:36:57,360 --> 00:37:01,720
 to the patient's actual anatomy.

415
00:37:01,720 --> 00:37:06,520
 And here's this concept of mutual protection so we talked all about how the front teeth

416
00:37:06,520 --> 00:37:12,640
 protect the back teeth and that's in protrusive movements and lateral movements with that incisal

417
00:37:12,640 --> 00:37:18,280
 guidance that canine guidance that's how we can protect the back teeth from feeling all

418
00:37:18,280 --> 00:37:23,800
 of that wear from sliding whereas the back teeth also protect the front teeth because

419
00:37:23,800 --> 00:37:30,360
 the back teeth have flat occlusal surfaces and strong roots, nice divergent multiple roots

420
00:37:30,360 --> 00:37:35,760
 to help protect the anterior teeth from those up and down biting forces.

421
00:37:35,760 --> 00:37:41,640
 So it's really cool this concept of mutual protection how the front protects the back

422
00:37:41,640 --> 00:37:46,200
 and also the back protects the front.

423
00:37:46,200 --> 00:37:52,440
 Alright so that's about it for this video I'm sorry it went on for so long but I was

424
00:37:52,440 --> 00:37:59,680
 getting excited this is really cool information and central concepts to how the prosthodontic

425
00:37:59,680 --> 00:38:05,000
 world and prosthodontic treatment planning is working out there.

426
00:38:05,000 --> 00:38:12,000
 So just as a summary to review much of the things that I talked about we captured teeth

427
00:38:12,000 --> 00:38:18,400
 with Alginib and we pour the diagnostic casts, we capture the bite which is either MI or

428
00:38:18,400 --> 00:38:27,400
 CR with wax or PBS and with a bite registration, we capture the relationship between the maxilla

429
00:38:27,400 --> 00:38:33,200
 and the skull with a face bow and then with all of those information we can mount to an

430
00:38:33,200 --> 00:38:38,240
 appropriate articulator and then we can use that mounted case to study occlusion and

431
00:38:38,240 --> 00:38:44,080
 disclusion for that particular patient which can then inform how we design the prosthodontic

432
00:38:44,080 --> 00:38:45,720
 teeth.

433
00:38:45,720 --> 00:38:49,280
 Thanks so much for watching guys I really hope you enjoyed this video and we'll see you

434
00:38:49,280 --> 00:38:50,120
 in the next one.

